WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:03.060
We could have had so much more smoking, pranks,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:03.060 --> 00:00:07.080
and escaping the grind of the 9-to-5&nbsp;
day with a proposed "Workaholics" movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:07.080 --> 00:00:12.702
but that plan went up in smoke. Why cancel a&nbsp;
project two years in development? Keep watching.

00:00:12.720 --> 00:00:18.600
Unlike other workplace sitcoms like "Newsradio,"&nbsp;
"The Office," or "30 Rock," "Workaholics" wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:22.680
spearheaded by a famous comedian or a&nbsp;
longtime veteran producer. The series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:22.680 --> 00:00:27.120
had a strange road from concept to smash&nbsp;
hit, with much of its development coming&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:31.080
down to sheer luck and happenstance, which&nbsp;
is what made it such a great success story.

00:00:31.080 --> 00:00:34.140
Show creators Anders Holm,&nbsp;
Adam DeVine, Blake Anderson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.140 --> 00:00:39.240
and Kyle Newacheck were all struggling young&nbsp;
comedians, each navigating various ups and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.240 --> 00:00:43.320
downs in their careers by the mid-2000s.&nbsp;
Together they created Mail Order Comedy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:48.480
posting sketches on the early days of YouTube,&nbsp;
beginning in 2006. This brought them to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:48.480 --> 00:00:52.920
attention of Comedy Central, but surprisingly,&nbsp;
they didn't even realize the network was making&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.920 --> 00:00:57.780
overtures until it was almost too late.&nbsp;
DeVine told Collider in a 2013 interview,

00:01:04.920 --> 00:01:08.580
As DeVine tells it, Comedy Central&nbsp;
invited the group to pitch a series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:12.600
and within a year, a pilot was on the&nbsp;
air. But Holm made clear that it wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.600 --> 00:01:15.480
as simple as it might sound. Kyle Newacheck added,

00:01:28.680 --> 00:01:33.840
Soon after it began airing in 2011,&nbsp;
"Workaholics" found a devoted fan following.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.840 --> 00:01:39.660
With its signature brand of scattershot laughs,&nbsp;
off-the-wall antics, and meandering storylines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:39.660 --> 00:01:44.520
it appealed to just about anyone who'd ever&nbsp;
experienced the drudgery of a 9-to-5 job.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:44.520 --> 00:01:48.960
More than just your standard stoner comedy,&nbsp;
it was celebrated for never dreaming too big,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:53.400
and never jumping the shark. That's why its&nbsp;
humble conclusion was all the more appropriate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:53.400 --> 00:01:56.280
with a finale that eschewed&nbsp;
the typical grand goodbye.

00:01:56.280 --> 00:02:01.500
But the series didn't conclude in 2017 because&nbsp;
of low ratings, disappointment from studio heads,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:07.140
or bad reviews. Like some of the best shows in&nbsp;
TV history, the series bowed out because its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.140 --> 00:02:11.580
stars and creators wanted to go out on their&nbsp;
own terms. In a statement at the end of 2016,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.580 --> 00:02:15.660
when they made it known that "Workaholics"&nbsp;
would be ending after its seventh season,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:15.660 --> 00:02:21.360
Anderson, DeVine, Holm, and Newacheck made&nbsp;
clear that it was by choice. They told Deadline,

00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:36.840
With their "Workaholics" journey complete,&nbsp;
the four creators of the series moved on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.840 --> 00:02:41.340
to other projects. But outside of "Game&nbsp;
Over, Man!" and "The Package," a pair of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:45.000
Netflix original films in 2018, each of&nbsp;
them more or less did their own thing.

00:02:45.000 --> 00:02:48.300
DeVine acted in the series "The&nbsp;
Righteous Gemstones," Anderson&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:52.920
joined the 2020 Hulu series "Woke," while&nbsp;
Holm did a series of guest spots on shows&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:52.920 --> 00:02:56.580
like "The Unbreakable Kimmy Schmidt"&nbsp;
and "Mixed-Ish." Newacheck had been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.580 --> 00:02:59.520
penning scripts for Jemaine Clements&nbsp;
"What We Do in the Shadows" series.

00:02:59.520 --> 00:03:05.220
Throughout 2021 and 2022, work progressed on&nbsp;
crafting the series' return to television.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:05.220 --> 00:03:09.780
Planned as an original streaming movie&nbsp;
that would air exclusively on Paramount+,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.780 --> 00:03:14.520
there was tangible excitement for a follow-up.&nbsp;
The show's Twitter account fired up and began&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.520 --> 00:03:19.140
posting recollections of the series' best&nbsp;
moments, and the four creators launched their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.140 --> 00:03:23.400
own podcast "This Is Important," which every&nbsp;
so often teased out their work on the project.

00:03:23.400 --> 00:03:29.820
But everything would come crashing down in January&nbsp;
of 2023, after nearly two years of development&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:29.820 --> 00:03:34.740
and pre-production, when Paramount+ announced&nbsp;
the film was being hit with the cancel hammer.

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:38.520
In an Instagram post, Adam DeVine&nbsp;
made it clear that there were no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:40.800
warning signs and no troubling developments as the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:44.160
production ramped up before they had the&nbsp;
rug pulled out from under them. He said,

00:03:55.920 --> 00:03:58.980
On the January 10th episode of&nbsp;
the "This Is Important" podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.980 --> 00:04:03.720
the gang went further, describing just how&nbsp;
sudden the announcement was. DeVine said,

00:04:03.720 --> 00:04:10.200
"We get a call from our manager Isaac that&nbsp;
Paramount+ is pulling the 'Workaholics' movie."

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:13.500
DeVine then went on to speak about&nbsp;
potential next steps for the movie.

00:04:13.500 --> 00:04:16.800
So, I think our goal is to take&nbsp;
it to Netflix or Hulu or Amazon&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.800 --> 00:04:19.260
or another streamer that thinks we're worthwhile."

00:04:19.680 --> 00:04:25.860
As unexpected as the news was, perhaps even more&nbsp;
odd was the timing. Production was so close,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.400
rehearsals were underway. And&nbsp;
with the four creators warmed up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.400 --> 00:04:33.360
its premature termination was all&nbsp;
the more dispiriting. DeVine said,

00:04:33.360 --> 00:04:40.800
"We were ready to go, we were doing table reads,&nbsp;
we have offices set up. We were building stage."

00:04:40.800 --> 00:04:43.260
Kyle Newachek added to the story by saying,

00:04:43.260 --> 00:04:44.580
"The stages are up, they're,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.580 --> 00:04:49.020
it's happening. The office was taped out&nbsp;
on the floor. Like, I saw it yesterday."

00:04:49.020 --> 00:04:51.780
Blake Anderson added to the conversation with his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.780 --> 00:04:54.540
thoughts talking about the crew that&nbsp;
had been working on the production,

00:04:54.540 --> 00:04:56.820
"It was, it was feeling very magical."

00:04:56.820 --> 00:05:00.000
DeVine finished the trip down&nbsp;
memory lane by concluding,

00:05:10.500 --> 00:05:15.780
As for why Paramount decided to pull the plug at&nbsp;
the 11th hour, it's not a question with a simple&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.780 --> 00:05:19.800
answer. In the first reports that came out about&nbsp;
the film being canned, The Hollywood Reporter&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:19.800 --> 00:05:24.300
claimed that a source had indicated the production&nbsp;
had suffered delays, and "it no longer fit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:24.300 --> 00:05:29.520
within the streamer's schedule." But Adam DeVine&nbsp;
relayed an entirely different, if somewhat vague,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:34.260
reasoning that he was given by the studio, saying&nbsp;
on Instagram that, quote, "[Paramount+] told us&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.260 --> 00:05:39.540
we don't fit their new 'global' strategy." But&nbsp;
what does that mean exactly? DeVine explained,

00:05:48.480 --> 00:05:51.480
The gang went on to discuss the&nbsp;
theory that their series doesn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.480 --> 00:05:55.260
resonate outside of the United States.&nbsp;
Because of an apparent shift towards&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.260 --> 00:05:59.340
programming that is popular across the&nbsp;
world, "Workaholics" can't compete.

00:05:59.340 --> 00:06:02.520
Unhappy to hear that the studio didn't&nbsp;
believe in the popularity of the series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:02.520 --> 00:06:06.120
the studio's reasoning didn't sit&nbsp;
well with Holm, Anderson, DeVine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:06.120 --> 00:06:10.740
and Newacheck, who contrasted their situation&nbsp;
with other recent revivals from Paramount.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:10.740 --> 00:06:14.280
"They made a 'Reno 911' movie, what&nbsp;
happened to the 'Workaholics' movie?"

00:06:14.940 --> 00:06:18.780
As you might imagine, the cast and creators&nbsp;
of "Workaholics" weren't pleased when they&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:18.780 --> 00:06:21.420
got word that the upcoming movie&nbsp;
was being ditched. Though Adam&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.420 --> 00:06:25.800
DeVine's note on Instagram struck a&nbsp;
mostly professional, if annoyed tone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.800 --> 00:06:30.120
they aired their grievances with much more&nbsp;
frankness on their podcast, spending the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.120 --> 00:06:35.340
majority of the two episodes after cancellation&nbsp;
expounding on the ill-fated film. DeVine said,

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:40.901
"Nothing! No matter what they throw&nbsp;
at us, we'll keep chugging along."

00:06:40.901 --> 00:06:45.240
Nevertheless, it appears to be an uphill climb&nbsp;
for all involved. According to IndieWire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:45.240 --> 00:06:48.600
Paramount Global chief programming&nbsp;
officer of streaming Tanya Giles&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:53.400
said at TCA on January 9th that, "We're&nbsp;
big fans of the 'Workaholics' guys." Yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:53.400 --> 00:06:57.540
she backed up DeVine's explanation that the&nbsp;
film didn't fit within their global strategy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:02.580
The project remains with MTV Entertainment&nbsp;
Studios, and its future is uncertain.

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:06.780
Though little concrete info had&nbsp;
found its way out of the production,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.780 --> 00:07:10.260
there are some tidbits regarding what the&nbsp;
creators of "Workaholics" may have had&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:10.260 --> 00:07:14.940
planned. Upon its announcement in February&nbsp;
of 2021, MTV Entertainment Group president&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:19.440
Chris McCarthy spoke with Vulture about&nbsp;
the then-upcoming film. Along with several&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.440 --> 00:07:23.910
other relaunched shows like "Beavis and&nbsp;
Butthead," "Inside Amy Schumer," and "Reno&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:23.910 --> 00:07:28.080
911," the new movie would have been part of&nbsp;
a "Comedy Central Hub" that he claimed would,

00:07:37.200 --> 00:07:40.740
But beyond nebulous corporate&nbsp;
speak, McCarthy added that the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.740 --> 00:07:43.620
"Workaholics" film would center on&nbsp;
"what it's like to work through the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:43.620 --> 00:07:47.640
pandemic." Given how the workplace has&nbsp;
changed since 2020 thanks to COVID-19,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:47.640 --> 00:07:51.900
there would have been no shortage of fresh&nbsp;
material to use as fodder for the film.

00:07:51.900 --> 00:07:55.140
But that's not all, as it seems they&nbsp;
were even putting pieces in place to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.140 --> 00:08:00.101
use the film as a backdoor pilot for a full&nbsp;
series revival. McCarthy said at the time,

00:08:06.660 --> 00:08:10.440
Just because Paramount+ has tapped&nbsp;
out on making a "Workaholics'' movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:10.440 --> 00:08:14.460
that doesn't mean it's truly done. The four&nbsp;
creators aren't giving up. On their podcast,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.460 --> 00:08:19.320
they discussed plans to shop the movie around.&nbsp;
They made a point that because sets, stages,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.320 --> 00:08:24.600
and crew are all still in place, at least for the&nbsp;
moment, they're primed to be picked up quickly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:24.600 --> 00:08:27.960
if a smart studio is willing to step&nbsp;
up to the plate. Newacheck said,

00:08:28.141 --> 00:08:32.281
"If there's any friends of&nbsp;
the pod that have big budgets,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.281 --> 00:08:35.580
you know, studios, streamers, we're ready to go."

00:08:35.580 --> 00:08:40.500
It may seem unlikely, but it wouldn't be&nbsp;
unprecedented for a canceled project to be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:40.500 --> 00:08:45.360
dropped by one network and quickly snatched up by&nbsp;
another. Netflix in particular has a history of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.360 --> 00:08:50.460
picking up canceled shows, including new seasons&nbsp;
of "Longmire" and "Manifest." They also grabbed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:50.460 --> 00:08:55.200
the animated tale "The Little Prince" in 2015 just&nbsp;
two weeks before it was dropped by none other than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:59.700
Paramount. Given the gang's strong relationship&nbsp;
with Netflix, who they worked with on "Game Over,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:59.700 --> 00:09:04.320
Man!," it's at least a possibility that&nbsp;
the "Workaholics" movie isn't dead yet.

00:09:04.980 --> 00:09:10.740
Now that the "Workaholics" movie is a no-go,&nbsp;
Kyle, Adam, Blake, and Anders are facing some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:10.740 --> 00:09:14.100
serious time off that had been earmarked&nbsp;
for producing the film. While they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:18.660
undoubtedly bummed to not be rolling cameras&nbsp;
on the revival, that may not be what they're&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:18.660 --> 00:09:23.100
most upset about. It sounds like they're most&nbsp;
unhappy about not spending the spring together,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.100 --> 00:09:26.940
something they noted they haven't done&nbsp;
in quite some time. DeVine acknowledged,

00:09:26.940 --> 00:09:30.660
"I was excited to hang out with my&nbsp;
boys for a few months straight."

00:09:30.660 --> 00:09:34.200
But they're not about to sit around doing&nbsp;
nothing and just waiting for something to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.200 --> 00:09:38.220
come up, as they're too pumped up to sit&nbsp;
on their hands. Holm suggested they might&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.220 --> 00:09:41.820
crank out a quick indie movie together&nbsp;
and capitalize on the opening in their&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.820 --> 00:09:45.840
schedule. The notion of a live "This is&nbsp;
Important" podcast tour was also raised.

00:09:45.840 --> 00:09:50.820
Hearing them spitball ideas, it's obvious that&nbsp;
the gang wants to do something big with the time&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:50.820 --> 00:09:56.280
off. What that will wind up being is anyone's&nbsp;
guess, but an improv show or a low-budget,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.280 --> 00:09:59.160
straight-to-streaming original&nbsp;
movie seems like a possibility.

00:09:59.880 --> 00:10:04.320
Though it may have come as a shock to fans of&nbsp;
"Workaholics" when Paramount dropped the film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.320 --> 00:10:08.160
it's nothing new in Hollywood. It's certainly&nbsp;
not the first time a movie was announced,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:08.160 --> 00:10:12.660
only to never make it to the screen. Even late&nbsp;
into production, plenty of shows have been&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:12.660 --> 00:10:16.680
canceled before going in front of the camera,&nbsp;
and some even after they had begun shooting.

00:10:16.680 --> 00:10:21.660
Notably, the announced relaunch of '80s legal&nbsp;
drama classic "L.A. Law" was put out to pasture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:21.660 --> 00:10:27.420
by ABC in May of 2022, two years after a major&nbsp;
announcement of a legacy sequel with some of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:27.420 --> 00:10:32.760
original cast. Similarly, the proposed revival of&nbsp;
'90s stalwart "Coach" was quickly canned in August&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:32.760 --> 00:10:38.460
of 2015, despite receiving a straight-to-series&nbsp;
order by NBC. It was in the middle of production.

00:10:38.460 --> 00:10:45.300
Perhaps most famously however, "Star Trek: Phase&nbsp;
II," a planned mid-'70s revival of the iconic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.300 --> 00:10:51.060
'60s series, was all set to go, sets were even&nbsp;
constructed and a cast was in place, when the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.060 --> 00:10:55.500
studio pulled the plug. Ultimately, they chose&nbsp;
to pivot to a big-screen feature film instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:55.500 --> 00:11:01.020
which wound up sparking further series to come.&nbsp;
So, perhaps there's hope for "Workaholics" yet.

